content.text:
The structure breaks into three parts.

**Right side.** A benzene ring carries an ethoxy group (CH3CH2–O) on one carbon. The adjacent ring carbon bears a C(=O)NH–CH2– amide group, giving a 2-ethoxybenzamide.

**Middle.** The CH2 is attached to C4 of an oxazole ring. Going around the ring: O1, C2, N3, C4, C5. C2 carries the aryl group, N3 is double-bonded to C2, and C4 is double-bonded to C5.

**Left side.** A benzene ring is attached to oxazole C2 at one carbon. From that carbon, the ring carbons are:
- the next carbon bears the isopropoxy group (OCH(CH3)2);
- the one after that bears the OCHF2 group.

Reading the drawing, the ring vertices are:
- C1 (600,882), attached to the oxazole;
- C2 (608,762), a CH;
- C3 (506,694), bearing the isopropoxy group;
- C4 (397,748), bearing the OCHF2 group;
- C5 (390,868), a CH;
- C6 (492,936), a CH.

So the ring is 3-isopropoxy-4-(difluoromethoxy)phenyl.

SMILES: CCOc1ccccc1C(=O)NCc1coc(n1)-c1ccc(OC(F)F)c(OC(C)C)c1
